{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01UBEYMsNbmZ5SmYXfb3aTkc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context and propose tasks for release 02.1 (tee).\n\nFirst, let me understand the current state:\n- Release 02.1 is \"Batch 2.1 — tee specification and differential test\"\n- It has one use case: rel02.1-uc001-tee with specstatus: spec_complete, codestatus: not started\n- I need to find the PRD for tee\n\nLooking at the analysis, rel02.1 has spec_complete status and code readiness \"none\". The use case is rel02.1-uc001-tee.\n\nI need to propose tasks for implementing tee. Since I can only propose 1 task max, and I need to split implementation and tests, I should propose the implementation task first.\n\n\nNow I'm reviewing what tee does — it's a straightforward utility that reads from stdin and writes to both stdout and specified files, with support for flags like -a for append, -i to ignore SIGINT, and the GNU --output-error extension. The implementation will need to handle SIGPIPE through pkg/sys and use pkg/testutils for differential testing against a reference binary.\n\nSince I don't have the actual PRD file yet, I'm working from the naming convention I see in other PRDs to figure out what number tee would be assigned. The implementation itself is simple enough — just cmd/tee/main.go with corresponding differential tests — but given the constraint about splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks, I should focus the single task on the implementation first. I'm stuck on the exact PRD filename for tee since I can't explore the filesystem, but I know it follows the pattern `docs/specs/product-requirements/prdXXX-tee.yaml` and I can reference the use case and test suite files instead to guide the stitch agent toward the right context. I'm realizing the stitch agent needs exact file paths in the required_reading field, but I don't have the actual PRD number for tee. Looking at the numbering gaps and the release roadmap, tee should be somewhere between prd004 and prd013, but without being able to verify the files, I'll need to reference it by a reasonable guess or use a pattern that captures the likely path. I'm realizing I need to be practical here—the stitch agent will need an actual PRD path, so I'll use a descriptive reference pattern that makes it clear what to look for rather than guessing at the exact numbering. I'm mapping out which PRD numbers are likely assigned to each utility based on their release versions—ts probably landed in the prd004-005 range during rel01.0, while the rel02.0 batch of simpler utilities might cluster together in the prd012+ range. Since I can't pin down tee's exact PRD number, I'll reference the use case and test suite instead, which should trace back to the PRD through their touchpoints.\n\nFor the tee implementation itself, it's straightforward: read from stdin, write to stdout and specified files, with support for append and interrupt-ignore flags plus GNU extensions for output error handling. The constraint about splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks means I should propose just the implementation task now, with tests coming later.\n\nI'll set up the required reading to point to the use case spec, test expectations, architecture documentation, and the SIGPIPE handler from pkg/sys—though I'll let the stitch agent track down the exact file names since I'm not certain of the module structure. the use case file. I'm settling on the core tee functionality plus the most important flags: stdin-to-stdout-and-files copying, the append flag, ignore-SIGINT, and output-error handling with its different modes. That's substantial enough for a 150-250 line implementation without overcomplicating things.\n\nNow I'm ready to draft the actual task. I've got the test suite path from the analysis data and I know the spec is marked complete, so I'll reference those and pull the PRD details from the use case traces.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27544,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27544},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"270c185a-581a-47da-8351-090fb909cc3a","uuid":"5e8049dd-f50d-484d-bdf2-ba3d3f7ddf80"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01UBEYMsNbmZ5SmYXfb3aTkc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** go-unix-utils is a specification-driven experiment that regenerates Unix utilities in Go, verified via differential testing against GNU reference binaries. The goal is to stress-test the mage-claude-orchestrator code generation pipeline.\n\n**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Human writes PRDs/use cases; orchestrator generates Go code in `cmd/` and `pkg/`; differential testing harness (`pkg/testutils`) verifies output parity against Homebrew GNU binaries.\n\n**Current state:** Specifications exist for many releases. Code for rel02.0 (true, false, yes, basename, dirname) plus pkg/sys, pkg/testutils was generated in run 16. Release 02.1 (tee) has spec_complete status with no code yet.\n\n**Current release target:** rel02.1 — one use case: `rel02.1-uc001-tee` (tee utility implementation).\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nRelease 02.1 is the earliest incomplete release in scope. tee is a straightforward stdin-to-files utility with `-a` (append), `-i` (ignore SIGINT), and `--output-error` GNU extension. It depends on `pkg/sys` (SIGPIPE handler) and `pkg/testutils` (differential testing), both already implemented. Per P2, implementation and tests are separate tasks. With a 1-task limit, the implementation task comes first.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"cmd/tee implementation: rel02.1-uc001-tee core functionality\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel02.1-uc001-tee.yaml (use case with PRD traceability)\n      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-02.1.yaml (expected test inputs and outputs)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (shared_protocols section for differential testing and SIGPIPE patterns)\n      - pkg/sys/ (InstallSIGPIPEHandler API -- call at start of main)\n      - cmd/true/main.go (reference for minimal cmd/ structure and flag parsing conventions)\n      - cmd/basename/main.go (reference for flag parsing and --version/--help pattern)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/tee/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: tee utility implementation -- stdin to stdout and files\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: |\n          Read all of stdin and write it to stdout and to each file argument\n          simultaneously. Use io.MultiWriter to fan out writes. Exit 0 on\n          success, exit 1 on any write error. When no file arguments are given,\n          tee copies stdin to stdout only.\n      - id: R2\n        text: |\n          Implement the -a/--append flag. When set, open output files with\n          O_APPEND instead of O_TRUNC. Default behavior (no -a) truncates\n          and overwrites each file.\n      - id: R3\n        text: |\n          Implement the -i/--ignore-interrupts flag. When set, install a\n          signal handler that ignores SIGINT (signal.Ignore(syscall.SIGINT)).\n          Without -i, tee uses default SIGINT behavior.\n      - id: R4\n        text: |\n          Implement --output-error with optional mode argument. Modes:\n          \"warn\" (warn on error to any output, continue), \"warn-nopipe\"\n          (warn on error to any output not a pipe, continue),\n          \"exit\" (exit on error to any output), \"exit-nopipe\" (exit on\n          error to any output not a pipe). When --output-error is given\n          without a mode value, default to \"warn\". Without --output-error\n          at all, tee diagnoses errors writing to any output except pipes\n          and exits after all inputs are processed if any error occurred.\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: |\n          Call sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at the start of main per the\n          shared SIGPIPE protocol in ARCHITECTURE.yaml. This ensures tee\n          exits cleanly when downstream consumers close their stdin.\n      - id: D2\n        text: |\n          Use flag parsing conventions consistent with the existing cmd/\n          packages (see cmd/basename or cmd/true for reference). Support\n          both short (-a, -i) and long (--append, --ignore-interrupts,\n          --output-error) flag forms. Implement --version and --help\n          following the same pattern as other cmd/ utilities.\n      - id: D3\n        text: |\n          Use os.OpenFile with appropriate flags (O_WRONLY|O_CREATE and\n          either O_TRUNC or O_APPEND) for each output file. Close all\n          files after stdin is fully consumed. Collect errors and report\n          them to stderr as \"tee: <filename>: <error>\".\n      - id: D4\n        text: |\n          Read stdin in a loop using a fixed buffer (e.g., 32 KB) and\n          write each chunk to all outputs. Do not read all of stdin into\n          memory at once. This matches GNU tee behavior for large inputs.\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: cmd/tee/main.go compiles with \"go build ./cmd/tee/\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"echo hello | go run ./cmd/tee/ prints hello to stdout\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"echo hello | go run ./cmd/tee/ /tmp/tee_test.txt writes hello to both stdout and /tmp/tee_test.txt\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"The -a flag appends to existing files instead of truncating\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"The -i flag causes SIGINT to be ignored\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"--output-error with mode values warn, warn-nopipe, exit, exit-nopipe behaves as specified\"\n      - id: AC7\n        text: \"golangci-lint runs clean on cmd/tee/\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27544,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27544},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"270c185a-581a-47da-8351-090fb909cc3a","uuid":"c48bc45c-ee5d-4a6e-90dd-97c1889832dc"}
